I think J.U.L.I.A. is bound to become a cult classic- this is my take on why this title is so special:
- Impressive story (really- I mean it!);
- The story is always given to you with something concealed, in a way that the player must imagine and figure out part of it on his own- I consider it to be advance writing skills;
- You'll get surprised and twited by it's outcome;
- The puzzles are incredible well design and there's a good variety on them;
- The graphics and cutscenes are beautiful;
- The soundtrack marry perfectly with the game;
- The voice acting is superb
I think this is a must play- as it's one of the most surprising games I have ever touched.
I won't give a 10 because I think it lacks some minor details- like keyboard shortcuts, sometimes the points of interactions are too small and the texts are also a little bit too small. But like a said, those are minor detail- this title is way too impressive.
(9.5 out of 10)

A beatiful, short, death-positive look at the funeral industry.
The core loop of the game is straightforward: you get an email about the deceased and how they (or their family) wish their remains tended to. You take care of the body (be it burial or cremation) through one or two point-and-click events. It's simply, but it gets the job of conveying the process down; Did you know we don't turn to ash when cremated? Nope, first you cook, then your bones are put through a cremulator, which sounds like a Transformer.
Understand that this isn't a Choice Your Own Adventure, but a window into the life of a funeral technician, Charlie. Through emails with colleagues and friends, you learn more about the business, and thanks to a newsletter your friend Jen signed you up to, the world of funerals. Don't ignore that second tab, though, unless you want to miss some of the events happening around Charlie (and a spookier version of Minesweeper).
The length/price of the game may put some off, but in its favour:
- it doesn't outstay its welcome;
- the artstyle is lovely without being morbid;
- it shows you the funeral processes for burial and cremation (while teaching some of their variations);
- the core loop is solid without becoming rote, and;
- It has Jen with her wine and millions of open tabs
Please check it out however you can.
P.S. Chad Grant needs to get in the sea. <3

