This is one of those games you shouldn't play alone for maximum enjoyment. My friends and I were just hanging out, thinking about what movie to watch, and then I remembered that this existed and looked quite fun. And we've had a blast!
PROS:
* The acting is hillarious. It's not "amazing" by some standards, but the actors all seem to have a blast playing their parts, and that is very enjoyable to watch (to name a few: Ryan alway taking a drink after finishing a dialogue, Paul almost literally spitting poison as he speaks and inspector Jenks'... uhm... "interesting" ways of asking certain questions);
* Really atmospheric environments;
* Pretty good soundtrack, adding to the atmosphere;
* Really good FMV quality;
* Can be enjoyed on a "weaker" PC (most people with high-end PCs seem to forget the importance of this aspect. Not everyone can afford a "monster", so it's good such games are still being made. Same goes for retro-style / pixelated games);
* While not the best detective story, it's still solid and kept me interested, intrigued and wanting to play through it.
PROS (of playing with someone):
* This game isn't about gameplay, so it doesn't matter who's controlling the mouse;
* Anyone can get up, go grab some more pizza/snacks or go to the bathroom in between ANY dialogue without missing a thing;
* The plot moves along smoothly because more people are thinking together and noticing things (we've each got a fair number of contradictions right, so everyone was involved);
* More engaging than just watching a movie;
* It's way more fun making detective Jenks run up and down the stairs behind the farm.
CONS:
* Some of the contradictions seemed a little odd and not quite fitting while others that were (in our minds) obviously fitting - weren't at all;
* The story seemed less realistic and more far-fetched at times;
* Minor bugs and glitches here and there (we couldn't stop questioning a person at one point because the cursor "disappeared", so we've had to restart the game);
* You couldn't question people about the screwdriver :( (for some reason this was a big issue to us).
So yes, most of the pros don't matter if you play this game alone. All in all it's a neat idea and a great thing to socially experience with people who are into this sort of stuff.
TL;DR
Play it with friends on a couch > play it alone

This game reminds me of my favorite things to do on Source-based games and Mirror's Edge: Running and bhopping.
// Gameplay
The main element of the game is to try and speedrun to the end of the level, however possible. It might seem strange at first, but once you understand the controls it's a smooth learning experience. For those with past experience with bhopping, be aware that this game is simpler in style than your go-to CS:Source bhop server. There is a speed limit at 1,250 units per second, no speed is gained from circle jumping or strafing in mid-air, and bhops are easy to do.
Despite its simplicity (in contrast to older games alike), an interesting difference is that the best approach to a faster speedrun is going in a straight line, as opposed to bhopping in Source engine games where you need to strafe between every jump to gain speed. It feels like a combination of the smooth mouse movement in surfing, and the constant jumps from bhopping.
Finally, the game also has double jumping. The timing is tricky: jump too early and you go too high, too late and you barely ascend at all. A great feature in my opinion, it adds for variety when looking for the optimal route in a level.
// Visuals
The game was created in Unreal Engine 4, and it looks
. The graphic options, though, are few and simple. There's no choice to disable motion blur, which can be distracting, as well as bloom effects. Framerate can be capped at several intervals, the UI has both a simple setting with just speed and time, and an advanced one that offers some more data on the top-left corner.
The overall tone is somewhat dark, with lights on walls and floors to light up the big, spacious rooms where you run. (At least the stock ones, we'll have to see what people create and upload to the workshop).
// Sound
Not much to be said, although the sound when you bhop reminds me of the "Uh" from when you jump in Doom. A soundtrack would be nice, but for now, there's no background music aside from the menu.
// Bugs
Minimal: sometimes you hear a thud when landing on a platform even though it's already too late to jump off of it, sometimes sliding results in no running sound after you stop sliding.
// Overall
I adore games that are simple to pick up but difficult to master, because this means that the gameplay is balanced and carefully planned. When properly executed, you get classics like Rocket League, Portal, and the like. Having experience in this kind of games, I don't need more than an hour of playing Inertia to be able to tell that I'll be enjoying it and discovering everything it has to offer. If the developers are careful enough to listen to their audience, and devoted enough to keep adding content to the game, this will no doubt become the game that those who reminisce in their memories of running in source games go to.
Four bucks means it's a steal, but a game this good on its day of release AND on early access? Go ♥♥♥♥ing buy it.

